            "About a quarter of married Ghanaian women use a modern method and about one in five who want to avoid pregnancy are using nothing — the <strong>unmet need</strong> that shows up as unplanned pregnancies, unsafe abortions, short birth intervals and adolescent births. Spacing births at least <strong>24 months</strong> apart (WHO advice; the national message is two to three years) lowers the risk of preterm birth, low birth weight, maternal anaemia and under-five death; avoiding pregnancy before 18 and after 35, and limiting high parity, removes some of the highest-risk pregnancies altogether. Family planning is offered at every contact — the antenatal clinic, immediately after birth, the postnatal and child welfare clinics, after a miscarriage or abortion and at every adolescent-friendly corner — and under the National Health Insurance Scheme family planning services have been brought into the benefit package so that cost is a smaller barrier than it was.",
            "The nurse's job is <strong>informed choice</strong>, not persuasion. The <strong>GATHER</strong> steps structure a session: <strong>G</strong>reet the client and ensure privacy; <strong>A</strong>sk about her situation, plans, partner, breastfeeding, medical history and what she already knows or fears; <strong>T</strong>ell her about the methods that suit her, starting with what she asks about; <strong>H</strong>elp her choose; <strong>E</strong>xplain how to use the method, its side effects and warning signs, ideally with a demonstration and written or pictorial instructions; and arrange <strong>R</strong>eturn visits and tell her she can come back at any time, for any reason, including to change method. Counselling that concentrates on the client's own priorities — effectiveness, return of fertility, privacy from a partner, bleeding pattern, protection from infection, cost and how often she must visit — is what keeps women on a method. A woman undecided between methods needs her own goals clarified, not the provider's favourite.",
            "The <strong>WHO Medical Eligibility Criteria (MEC)</strong> grade every method against every condition: <strong>category 1</strong> no restriction, <strong>category 2</strong> benefits generally outweigh risks, <strong>category 3</strong> risks usually outweigh benefits (use only if nothing else is acceptable, with specialist judgement), <strong>category 4</strong> unacceptable risk. The MEC wheel or app is the reference; the conditions that matter most in daily practice are listed with each method below. Before starting any hormonal method you must be <strong>reasonably sure the client is not pregnant</strong>: she has no symptoms or signs, and she is within 7 days of the start of a period, within 4 weeks of giving birth (if not breastfeeding), fully breastfeeding and amenorrhoeic under 6 months postpartum, within 7 days of a miscarriage or abortion, has had no sex since her last period, or has been using another reliable method correctly. If none applies, a pregnancy test (negative two weeks or more after the last unprotected sex) or starting the method and returning for a test are the options — do not simply send her away to wait for a period, because that is when the pregnancy happens.",
