            "Gynaecological problems arrive as one of a few complaints, and each has a short list. <strong>Abnormal bleeding</strong> — heavy periods (menorrhagia: soaking a pad hourly, clots, flooding, anaemia), bleeding between periods, after sex or after the menopause — points to fibroids, polyps, adenomyosis, anovulatory cycles of adolescence and the perimenopause, hormonal methods, infection, pregnancy complications, and the cancers of the cervix and endometrium; <strong>bleeding after sex or after the menopause is cancer until proven otherwise</strong>. <strong>Pain</strong> — cyclical pain with periods (primary dysmenorrhoea in a teenager, which responds to NSAIDs started at the onset of bleeding, heat and exercise, and the COC; secondary dysmenorrhoea from endometriosis, adenomyosis, fibroids or infection in an older woman), deep pain with intercourse (dyspareunia — endometriosis, pelvic infection), sudden one-sided pain (ovarian cyst torsion or rupture, ectopic pregnancy) and chronic pelvic pain (infection, endometriosis, adhesions). <strong>A lump or swelling</strong> — fibroids, an ovarian cyst, a full bladder, pregnancy, and the rare ovarian cancer that presents late with bloating and a swollen abdomen. <strong>Discharge</strong> — physiological, candida, bacterial vaginosis, trichomonas, cervicitis from gonorrhoea or chlamydia, and a foul bloodstained discharge from a retained foreign body or a cancer. <strong>Leakage of urine or faeces</strong> — stress incontinence and prolapse after childbirth, or continuous leakage from a fistula. <strong>Failure to conceive</strong>, <strong>amenorrhoea</strong> and <strong>menopausal symptoms</strong> complete the list. Every woman of reproductive age with pain or bleeding has a pregnancy test before anything else is decided.",
            "<strong>Fibroids (leiomyomas)</strong> — benign smooth-muscle tumours, present in up to half of Black women by 40 and the commonest reason for gynaecological surgery in Ghana — cause heavy, prolonged periods with anaemia, pressure symptoms (frequency, constipation, a mass), pain if they degenerate or twist, and sometimes subfertility and miscarriage; they grow with oestrogen and shrink after the menopause. Management ranges from iron and tranexamic acid or NSAIDs for bleeding, the levonorgestrel IUS, GnRH analogues to shrink them before surgery, to <strong>myomectomy</strong> (preserving the uterus for a woman who wants children, with a significant bleeding risk) and <strong>hysterectomy</strong>. Pre-operatively correct anaemia, group and cross-match, confirm the woman understands what will and will not be removed and what it means for periods and fertility; post-operatively watch for haemorrhage (vaginal loss and the abdomen), urinary retention and ureteric or bladder injury, thromboembolism and infection, and prepare her for a 4–6 week recovery, pelvic rest and — after hysterectomy with oophorectomy — menopausal symptoms. <strong>Ovarian cysts</strong> are usually functional and resolve; a cyst that twists (torsion) or ruptures presents as sudden severe one-sided pain with vomiting and sometimes shock, and is a surgical emergency easily confused with ectopic pregnancy and appendicitis. <strong>Endometriosis</strong> — endometrial tissue outside the uterus — causes progressive painful periods, deep dyspareunia, pain opening the bowels or passing urine during periods and infertility; NSAIDs, continuous combined pills or progestogens, and laparoscopic treatment. <strong>Polycystic ovary syndrome</strong> presents as irregular or absent periods, hirsutism, acne, weight gain and subfertility, with later diabetes risk; weight loss, cycle regulation and ovulation induction. <strong>Uterovaginal prolapse</strong> — a dragging sensation, a lump at the vulva, urinary and bowel symptoms in a multiparous or older woman — is managed with pelvic floor exercises, a ring pessary (changed every 4–6 months) or surgery; prevention is good second-stage care and avoiding prolonged pushing.",
